I'll explain the problem with Active Record models that embed business rules in ORM lifecycle hooks and show how to refactor them using Clean Architecture principles.

## The Problem: Active Record with Embedded Business Logic

Here's an example of problematic Active Record code where business rules are tightly coupled to persistence:

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Business logic embedded in ORM model
class Order {
  private _status: OrderStatus;
  private _totalAmount: number;
  private _items: OrderItem[];
  private _customer: Customer;

  constructor(
    private _id: string,
    private _customerId: string,
    private _items: OrderItem[],
    private _status: OrderStatus = OrderStatus.PENDING
  ) {
    this._totalAmount = this.calculateTotal();
  }

  // ❌ Business logic mixed with persistence concerns
  beforeSave(): void {
    // Business rule: Order must have at least one item
    if (this._items.length === 0) {
      throw new Error("Order must have at least one item");
    }

    // Business rule: Order total must be positive
    if (this._totalAmount <= 0) {
      throw new Error("Order total must be positive");
    }

    // Business rule: Customer must be active
    if (!this._customer.isActive) {
      throw new Error("Cannot create order for inactive customer");
    }

    // Business rule: Status transition validation
    if (this._status === OrderStatus.CONFIRMED && this._status !== OrderStatus.PENDING) {
      throw new Error("Order can only be confirmed from PENDING status");
    }
  }

  // ❌ Persistence-specific logic mixed with domain logic
  async save(): Promise<void> {
    this.beforeSave(); // ← Business logic here!
    // Database persistence code...
  }

  private calculateTotal(): number {
    return this._items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price * item.quantity, 0);
  }
}
```

